3.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: In the canonical English and Russian texts the inspector's overcoat / шинель is not a background prop but a running barometer of Ochumelov’s opportunism. It is brought in, made anxious about, and ‘cleaned’ in step with the changes of opinion about the dog and the owner of the dog.

In the text that is usually printed that is four, essentially five, moments of the object.

* he comes on the scene “in a new overcoat / in a brand-new overcoat”;
* he is anxious not to soil the new overcoat with the dog / with dust;
* the newness / the soiling is remarked on again as he shifts from “the dog is a stray” to “the dog is General’s dog”;
* he brushes / shakes the dust off the overcoat as he re-shifts again.

Those four descriptive beats are the ones that are normally quoted as the coat-symbolising his fearful, chameleon mentality. The word шинель / overcoat is in fact repeated one more time in the closing exchange, so most editions count five verbal mentions of the garment, four of them being the symbolic descriptive shifts.

