6.text:
In the 1884 Russian text the шинель / пальто is not a background prop, it is the running visual shorthand for Ochumelov’s chameleon-like opportunism and it is put on the page only at the moments of the change of attitude.

In the text that is normally printed the garment is brought in and re-used on four stage-marks, which criticism habitually compresses to three substantive symbolic re-descriptions:

* Arrival/confident: Ochumelov rides up to the crowd in his long / new overcoat, buttoned up, the self-important uniform of the petty official who is going to ‘do something’ about the wounded dog;
* First fear/loosening: when the crowd suggests the dog may belong to a General he unbuttons / loosens the coat, fusses at the collar and worries about soiling the new overcoat with the dog / with dust, the physical marker of the first loss of self-assurance;
* Punitive re-buttoning: when it is suggested the dog is a cur / a butcher’s dog he pulls the coat tight again and reverts to the harsh punitive tone;
* Exit/settling: he leaves the scene with the coat again settled round him in its final pose, the chameleon back to its ‘normal’ colour.

That is four textual mentions of the шинель in the Russian original, four in most Russian editions and four – five lexical mentions of ‘overcoat / coat’ in the Constance Garnett / usual English version. The four references are normally read as three distinct symbolic deployments of the coat – arrive/assured → afraid of the General → angry/punitive → afraid again – i.e. three clear symbolic shifts of his fearful mentality, counted in the literature as the four descriptive episodes of the garment.

9.text:
The coat/shinel is not a decorative prop in the 1884 sketch, it is the external barometer of Ochumelov’s chameleon opportunism and it is re-activated every time his colour changes.

In the 1884 Russian original the garment is brought on to the scene with Ochumelov and then picked up again at each change of attitude. On the page that is four textual activations of the shinel:

* the arrival, “в новой шинели / в шинели”, the self-important petty official buttoned up and medalled, the assured/confident pose;
* the first deference, the coat is loosened / fussed with when the dog may be General Zhigalov’s and Ochumelov becomes apologetic and protective;
* the punitive reversal, the coat is pulled tight / re-buttoned when the dog is said to be a cur / a butcher’s dog and he reverts to harsh policeman;
* the final exit reversal, the coat is again loosened / settled round him as he leaves with the dog now again declared the General’s, the humiliated/retreating pose.

Those four stage-marks are habitually compressed in criticism into three substantive symbolic re-descriptions of the coat as a marker of fearful mentality – arrival/assured → afraid of the General → punitive confidence → afraid again – i.e. four mentions, three distinct symbolic moments of change. That is the four-mention / three-shift model that is quoted in the Russian original and that is reproduced as four, in most English editions five, lexical occurrences of overcoat/shinel in Garnett/Fell etc. 

If the arrival note is counted as a descriptive set-up and only the fear-changes are wanted, it is the three re-descriptions unbuttoned/deferential, re-buttoned/punitive, loosened/retreating that are normally cited as the coat = mentality barometer.

