1.text:
In the 1884 Russian printing the шинель is a running visual index and Chekhov puts it on the page only at the moments of colour change.

That is four textual stage-marks of the garment in the Russian original:

* arrival / baseline official pose – he comes up “в новой шинели”, buttoned up with the medal, the self-important petty authority;
* first wavering / deference – on the suggestion the dog may be General Zhigalov’s the coat is loosened/fussed with, he worries about soiling the new overcoat, the garment goes slack with fear;
* second reversal / punitive – when the dog is declared a cur/stray the coat is pulled tight/re-buttoned again and he reverts to harsh punitive bluster;
* exit / retreat – on departure the dust is brushed/shaken off the new overcoat and the garment is remarked upon again as he leaves humiliated/submissive.

Those four mentions are the four-beat pattern that is reproduced in English as five lexical occurrences of overcoat/coat in Garnett/Fell type translations.

Criticism habitually compresses the four textual pick-ups into three substantive symbolic re-descriptions of mentality, arrival assured → afraid of the General/deferential → punitive/aggressive → afraid again/retreating, i.e. three independent symbolic deployments of the coat as a barometer of fear, realised as four stage-marked mentions in Russian. The arrival note is the baseline from which the three shifts are measured; the fourth textual mention is a re-statement of the final fearful state.

That is the four-mention / three-shift reading on which the majority of us are converging.

