2.text:
In the 1884 Russian printing the шинель is used as a running visual barometer and it is only put on the page at the points of attitude change.

Textually that is four stage-marked activations of the garment in the Russian original, rendered as five lexical hits of overcoat/coat in Garnett/Fell English:

* Arrival / baseline official pose – he comes up “в новой шинели”, buttoned up with the medal showing, the self-important petty authority;
* First wavering / deference – on the suggestion the dog may be General Zhigalov’s the coat is loosened/fussed with, he is anxious not to soil the new шинель, the garment goes slack = fear/obsequiousness;
* Second reversal / punitive – when the dog is declared a cur/stray the coat is pulled tight/re-buttoned/re-adjusted and the punitive bluster returns;
* Exit / retreat – on reassertion of General ownership the dust is brushed/shaken off the new overcoat and the garment is remarked upon again as he leaves dishevelled/humiliated.

Those four textual pick-ups are the four-beat pattern everyone is citing. Criticism habitually compresses them into three substantive symbolic re-descriptions where Chekhov actually describes the coat to externalise the chameleon shifts of mentality:

confident buttoned baseline → loosened/deferential fear of the General → re-buttoned punitive confidence → loosened/retreating fear again.

The arrival “in a new overcoat” is the nominal costume set-up from which the shifts are measured and the exit brushing is a textual repeat of the second fearful state already described. That is the conventional four-mention / three-independent-symbolic-deployment reading: four mentions of шинель in the 1884 Russian, three independent descriptive-symbolic uses of the coat as a barometer of fearful, opportunistic mentality.

